WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.050
Our class garden grew beans,

2
00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:03.910
maize and tomatoes.

3
00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:06.740
Every day, pupils wrote what they

4
00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:08.120
picked on scraps of paper.

5
00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:10.610
Now the scraps are everywhere!

6
00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:15.100
Today: sort data into a table, so

7
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:16.980
it's easy to read and to count.

8
00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:21.460
A table sorts data into rows and columns.

9
00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:25.130
Rows go across, like the days of the week.

10
00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:28.210
Columns go down, like beans,

11
00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:30.130
maize and tomatoes.

12
00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:32.140
Each column has a heading.

13
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:34.840
Where a row meets a

14
00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:36.220
column, there's a cell.

15
00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:38.680
Each cell holds one fact:

16
00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:41.420
Monday, beans, six.

17
00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:44.330
One fact per cell, always.

18
00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:47.640
Here's one row of our garden table.

19
00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:51.320
On Monday we picked six beans, two

20
00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:53.800
maize cobs and four tomatoes.

21
00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:56.520
With a row for every day, we can

22
00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:57.920
see the whole week at once.

23
00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:00.620
Tables are easy to sort.

24
00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:03.430
Put the biggest first, and you see

25
00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:04.770
the best day straight away.

26
00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:08.150
Or sort by name, from A to Z.

27
00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:09.810
Quick check!

28
00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:12.690
In a table of pupils, what

29
00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:13.790
does one row show?

30
00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:15.780
One pupil.

31
00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:19.290
Each row is one thing, and each

32
00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:22.050
column is one fact about it, like

33
00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:23.730
age or favourite colour.

34
00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.320
Some tables confuse computers.

35
00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:29.750
A cell with two facts,

36
00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:31.370
like three and five.

37
00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:33.570
A Total row in the middle.

38
00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:36.310
Fix them, and the computer counts right.

39
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.340
In the app, Mr Bello's marks

40
00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:40.880
table looks fine to us.

41
00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.080
But the computer counts the pupils wrong!

42
00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:46.600
Tap a fix, and watch its

43
00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:47.740
answer turn right.

44
00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:49.520
Your turn.

45
00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:52.430
One cell says three and five:

46
00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:54.690
the beans and the tomatoes.

47
00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:55.880
What's wrong?

48
00:01:57.190 --> 00:01:59.910
Two facts in one cell: give beans

49
00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:01.690
and tomatoes a column each.

50
00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:04.610
Remember: rows go across,

51
00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:06.430
and columns go down.

52
00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:08.690
Every column needs a heading.

53
00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:11.510
And put one fact in each cell.

54
00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:14.440
Try it tomorrow: make a

55
00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:15.560
table of your week.

56
00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:18.100
One row for each day.

57
00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:20.460
Columns for the weather, and

58
00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:21.320
how you got to school.
